=================== #2557
AtlasProvider` mutations silently return `false` when called after `await authoriseUser()
assignees: 

Was noticed while working on Foresight unsubscribe that chains `authoriseUser` -> `deleteUser`.

## Summary

`addUser`, `updateEmail`, `deleteUser` and `uploadFile` guard on the `isVerified` **state** and close over a GraphQL client memoized on `authToken`. `authoriseUser()` only calls `setAuthToken(...)` — `isVerified` flips one render later, in an effect. So any consumer that chains sign-in into an action inside a single handler runs against the pre-sign-in closure: the guard returns `false`, no request is sent, and no error is thrown.

## Environment

- **Package**: `@kleros/kleros-app` 3.0.1
- **File**: `src/lib/atlas/providers/AtlasProvider.tsx`
- **React**: 18.3.1 (behaviour is not React-version-specific)

## Reproduction Steps

```tsx
const { isVerified, authoriseUser, deleteUser } = useAtlasProvider();

const handleUnsubscribe = async () => {
  if (!isVerified) {
    await authoriseUser();   // resolves; wallet signature accepted; token stored
  }
  const ok = await deleteUser();   // => false, and no network request is made
  if (!ok) showError("Unknown error");
};
```

1. Connect a wallet, land on a page in a signed-out state.
2. Press the button once.
3. Sign the SIWE message in the wallet — sign-in genuinely succeeds, the token is written.

## Expected Behavior

`deleteUser()` issues the `DeleteUser` mutation with the freshly-minted token, or throws something a caller can act on.

## Actual Behavior

`deleteUser()` returns `false` immediately. No GraphQL request is sent, no error is raised — the caller can only report a generic failure. Pressing the button a second time works, because by then a re-render has produced a fresh closure.

## Root Cause

The token lives in state, validity is *derived* from it asynchronously, and both the guard and the client are captured per-render:

```tsx
// L102 — token in state
const [authToken, setAuthToken] = useSessionStorage<string | undefined>("authToken", undefined);
// L108
const [isVerified, setIsVerified] = useState(false);

// L112-119 — client memoized on the token
const atlasGqlClient = useMemo(() => {
  const headers = authToken ? { authorization: `Bearer ${authToken}` } : undefined;
  return new GraphQLClient(`${config.uri}/graphql`, { headers });
}, [authToken, config.uri]);

// L140-166 — isVerified is only reconciled in an effect, a render later
useEffect(() => {
  const verifyAndSchedule = () => {
    setIsVerified(verifySession());
    ...
  };
  verifyAndSchedule();
  ...
}, [authToken, verifySession, address]);

// L235-254 — sign-in writes state and returns; nothing is available to the caller
const authoriseUser = useCallback(async (statement?: string) => {
  ...
  const token = await loginUser(atlasGqlClient, { message, signature });
  setAuthToken(token);          // <- the only effect of a successful sign-in
}, [address, chainId, setAuthToken, signMessageAsync, atlasGqlClient]);

// L298-310 — the guard reads the stale state, the client is the stale one
const deleteUser = useCallback(async () => {
  if (!address || !isVerified) return false;   // <- false in the sign-in closure
  ...
  await fetchWithAuthErrorHandling(() => deleteUserFromAtlas(atlasGqlClient));
}, [address, isVerified, setIsDeletingUser, atlasGqlClient, refetchUser]);
```

Two independent staleness bugs stack here. Even if the `isVerified` guard were bypassed, `atlasGqlClient` in that same closure still carries **no `Authorization` header**, so the request would fail as unauthenticated.

Same shape at `addUser` (L256-275), `updateEmail` (L277-296), `deleteUser` (L298-310) and `uploadFile` (L312+, which additionally reads `authToken` directly).

## Impact

**Medium** — every consumer hits this the first time it tries the natural "one button that signs in and then acts" flow, and the failure is silent (`false`, no throw), so it surfaces as an unexplained error toast. A workaround exists, but it is non-obvious and has to be reimplemented at every call site.

## Proposed Fix

Make the token readable synchronously and derive both the guard and the client from it at call time, rather than from per-render captures.

```tsx
const authTokenRef = useRef(authToken);
authTokenRef.current = authToken;          // keep in sync during render

// pure — no closure state
const isTokenValid = (token?: string, addr?: string) => { ... };

const buildClient = useCallback(
  (token?: string) =>
    new GraphQLClient(`${config.uri}/graphql`, {
      headers: token ? { authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } : undefined,
    }),
  [config.uri]
);

/** Returns an authenticated client, or null if the current token is unusable. */
const requireAuth = useCallback(() => {
  const token = authTokenRef.current;
  return isTokenValid(token, address) ? buildClient(token) : null;
}, [address, buildClient]);

const authoriseUser = useCallback(async (statement?: string) => {
  ...
  const token = await loginUser(atlasGqlClient, { message, signature });
  authTokenRef.current = token;   // <- synchronous, so a chained call sees it
  setAuthToken(token);
}, [...]);

const deleteUser = useCallback(async () => {
  const client = requireAuth();
  if (!client) return false;
  ...
}, [requireAuth, ...]);
```

`isVerified` stays exactly as it is for rendering; it just stops being load-bearing for request correctness.

A smaller, non-breaking alternative: have `authoriseUser` **return the token** it minted, and let the mutations accept an optional token override. That leaves the closure bug in place but at least gives callers a documented way out.

Either way it would help to distinguish "not authenticated" from "server said no" — today both are `false`, so callers cannot tell a caller-ordering mistake from a genuine backend rejection.

## Workaround (for other consumers hitting this)

Don't chain. Let sign-in only arm a flag, and run the mutation from a later render:

```tsx
const [pending, setPending] = useState(false);

useEffect(() => {
  if (!pending || !isVerified) return;
  setPending(false);
  void runMutation();          // fresh closure: token and flag are both live
}, [pending, isVerified, runMutation]);

// onPress
if (!isVerified) {
  await authoriseUser();
  setPending(true);
  return;
}
await runMutation();
```

Also reset the flag when `address` changes, or a wallet switch mid-sign-in leaves it armed for a session that will never verify.


